-- chem/h2two.hypercubical — two electrons and two protons on the closed cage of the diamond net: H₂ binds, for every
-- hop amplitude t > 0. Two protons and two electrons sum to zero charge, so the piece is closed and needs no wall
-- (chem/h2-net.hypercubical). A spatial state is a pair (u, v) of the electrons' vertices; the singlet is symmetric and
-- the two electrons may share a vertex. H = L·field-energy(protons; u, v) − t·(hop of electron 1 + hop of electron 2),
-- L = λ/2 (chem/charge.hypercubical's `field-energy`, chem/cover.hypercubical's hop). The separated-atom state φ₀ with the
-- electrons on the two protons (here 4 and 9) has field-energy 0, the lowest the Coulomb term reaches (`h2-vac`), and
-- no electron can hop without leaving a proton, so ⟨φ₀|H|φ₀⟩ = 0. A single hop of φ₀ moves one electron to a
-- neighbour of its proton: 4's neighbours are 0 and 1, 9's are 2 and 3, so the four one-hop states are (0,9), (1,9),
-- (4,2), (4,3), each with field-energy 3/4 (`h2-shell`) and each orthogonal to φ₀ and to the others (no two differ by
-- one hop). On the orthonormal pair (φ₀, φ₁ = ½Σ of the four) the Hamiltonian is [[0, −2t], [−2t, 3L/4]], whose
-- lowest eigenvalue (3L/4 − √(9L²/16 + 16t²))/2 is negative for every t > 0. So the molecular ground energy is below
-- 0, the energy of the two separated atoms, at every coupling: the molecule binds, and the binding is the hop's, the
-- off-diagonal −2t against the zero diagonal of the separated state. Checked: the vacuum and shell field-energies and
-- the proton degrees that count the shell; the 2×2 eigenvalue sign is the diagonal-zero determinant −4t². A leaf.
import "h2plus-net.hypercubical"

